Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:31.620 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  welcome to the Houghton library at  Harvard University I'm standing in the  entrance hall and behind me is the door  to the reading room where with proper  introductions you can come and look at  some of the most beautiful books and  manuscripts and objects you can imagine  there are various exhibition spaces I'm  standing in one of them there are others  upstairs but what I'd like you to see  with me is an exhibition that a seminar  at Harvard has produced that shows  Harvard's collection of medieval Scrolls  these are the scrolls that are talked  about in this module and so this  walkthrough of the exhibition can serve  us as an introduction to medieval  scrolls and to the scrolls in this  module or it can serve as a summary of  all the things that you may have looked  at in more detail in each of the other  units so in the largest of these  exhibition rooms the Edison and Newman  room we have laid out a collection of us  almost all of the scrolls that belong to  Harvard University the good news about  this whole thing is it provides a  wonderful representative sampling of the  kinds of Scrolls that survived to us  from the Middle Ages you'll see some  interesting things about scrolls in  general in this exhibition for one thing  a display case of this size isn't the  perfect way to display something that's  30 feet long so the majority of these  scrolls have to be kind of rolled up at  the ends which of course is how scrolls  are mostly used when they're long  nevertheless it makes it hard to examine  the whole thing so it's made an  interesting choice for us we've had to  pick what part of each scroll should be  shown to the people who go through you  lucky people have the opportunity to  examine all the surfaces of all these  Scrolls because they've all been very  carefully digitized by the Harvard  libraries  there's a number of things that jump to  the eye when you see these things in the  flesh in the parchment as it were one is  that they very enormous ly in size on a  computer screen everything's about the  same size but look at this great big  scroll here this is a universal  chronicle this is the only one that has  had to be displayed sideways because  it's wider than the display case itself  but it's a very handsome and very wide  this is the biggest of the scrolls we  have here and on the wall behind it is  this tiny little poetical scroll that  looks you can see to look at it even in  its frame that it was once folded but it  seems originally to have been a scroll  and is a poetical dialogue so very large  - very small but there are other things  that it's possible to notice - like  qualities of parchment look at this  handsome white parchment here and some  of the others you'll see much darker  very beat-up we also go from Scrolls  that are quite simple this is a scroll  in Greek with very little decoration two  Scrolls that are very highly decorated  this is a genealogy of English kings  that has almost more pictorial elements  than text to this very beautifully  decorated scroll of prayers with  decoration around the top and the  left-hand side almost as though it were  the left hand page in a codex you kind  of wonder why there's not decoration  down the right-hand margin and I think  it's because the decorator knows how you  do a decoration you go in the outer  margin in the top but nobody will know  for sure and then there's this scroll  here this very sad scroll this is a  chronicle and you can see that it once  had very beautiful pictures which are  now faded and that some great scholar no  doubt wanting to improve human knowledge  treated the text of this thing a hoping  that it would be possible to read it  better and the result has been to make a  mess of the parchment and of what was  once a very very beautiful scroll so the  variety of things that we see here and  the difference in types of parchment  size of scroll level of decoration level  of use and where all those things are  something that you can only see when  you're sort of walking through this  exhibition and looking almost touching  these very beautiful objects in this  module we talk about four kinds of  scroll not really kinds of scroll but  four reasons for making a scroll in the  Middle Ages when the standard way of  conveying a text was in a book in a  codex so I think we have in way examples  of almost all of those kinds of scroll  in this exhibition and looking at them  in those categories will allow us either  to introduce or to summarize the other  more detailed units that we've had one  reason for making the scroll is when you  don't know how long the scroll is going  to be until you're finished one that's a  little bit like that is is this scroll  over here this scroll is a legal scroll  and in a way it might be thought of as  the kind that could be extended as more  information as needed in fact it's a  copy of a treatise a treatise by a man  named Glanville about how to run a court  how to produce writs various kinds of  things and it is in the style of scroll  that in England was called a Chancery  role that is one that's made like all  these other roles by making it longer by  adding an additional piece of parchment  to the bottom of it the distinction was  made in England because other kinds of  Scrolls and this group of Scrolls here  is sort of the other way of making rolls  these are manor court roles they are  roles that record the legal actions in  the courts of English manners because  the lord of the manor was officially a  judge and his judgment was recorded in  rolls this kind of roll called Exchequer  rolls is made by stacking the parchment  up on top of each other and binding it  together across the top rather like a  kind of a flip chart in a classroom and  then rolling them up so big ones of  these are sometimes called  pipe rolls because they looked sort of  like a pipe so it's a very different  kind of roll from any of the other roles  we'll see here of this particular kind  of scroll so it could get longer as you  like but you do it by stacking the pages  up rather than adding them to the bottom  and so it's not really a roll it's  really a codex except for the fact that  they were stored rolled up so one kind  of scroll is the kind whose length is  not known  another reason for making a scroll is  when you want it to be portable when you  want to carry it around for in one place  to another and this little scroll on the  wall here is an example of that kind of  thing this is the smallest scroll at  Harvard it may not be the smallest  medieval scroll there is because there  were lots of other times when you wanted  something very small you might want a  life of saint margaret to wear around  your neck you might want to have a  little scroll of prayers that you  carried with you as a kind of an amulet  this one is actually a poetic dialogue  it's a fairly important text it's a  dialogue between Ganymede and Helen  about whether the love of men or the  love of women is better and you can see  along the sides the different characters  as they speak it's a scroll that once  was folded probably was intended to be  rolled and held in the hand while  reciting we know that actors very often  had a role actors still play roles and  the reason what actors do what's called  a role is because they held their part  on a role in their hand this is not  exactly an actor's role but it's the  kind of very small thing that poets are  often depicted as holding in their hands  and that we know actors did so the idea  of having it be small so it can be  portable there are other kinds of  portable scroll not all of which are  illustrated in this exhibition  sometimes a processional music for  churches you don't want to carry the  whole great big gradual around with you  so you write the necessary stuff on a  scroll to go carry carry around and  bless the crops or bless the different  churches of a city across the way here  is another scroll that we might think of  as being in the portable category  it's a series of prayers that are  designed to provide you time off from  purgatory this is a very beautiful  scroll and you can imagine that if if it  were rolled up it would be a little bit  smaller than this so it might be a nice  thing to carry around it might once also  have been hung on the wall or down low  for you to kneel down and pray but if  you say all these prayers you see that  they almost all begin with o if you say  all these prayers you get X number of  hundreds of years off in purgatory you  can see this scroll and hear it  discussed in one of the other one of the  other units but it seems clear that it's  small so that it can be carried around  because it's an object of personal  devotion

